2) Implementation of auto-fix for missing @story (REQ-AUTOFIX-MISSING, SAFE, PRESERVE)
- Rule module `src/rules/require-story-annotation.ts`:
  - References this story and requirements:
    ```ts
    /**
     * @story docs/stories/003.0-DEV-FUNCTION-ANNOTATIONS.story.md
     * @story docs/stories/008.0-DEV-AUTO-FIX.story.md
     * @req REQ-ANNOTATION-REQUIRED
     * @req REQ-AUTOFIX-MISSING - This rule supports auto-fixing missing @story annotations per Story 008.0 auto-fix behavior.
     * @req REQ-AUTOFIX-SAFE - Auto-fix behavior only inserts @story annotation JSDoc comments and never changes executable or runtime code.
     * @req REQ-AUTOFIX-PRESERVE - Auto-fix inserts a minimal placeholder JSDoc in a way that preserves existing surrounding formatting and structure.
     */
    ```
  - Declares auto-fix support:
    ```ts
    meta: {
      ...,
      hasSuggestions: true,
      fixable: "code",
      messages: { missingStory: "Function '{{name}}' must have an explicit @story annotation..." },
      ...
    }
    ```
- Helper `src/rules/helpers/require-story-helpers.ts` implements the actual fix and is wired to Story 008.0:
  - Fixed placeholder template (non-configurable, matching story scope):
    ```ts
    const STORY_PATH = "docs/stories/003.0-DEV-FUNCTION-ANNOTATIONS.story.md";
    const ANNOTATION = `/** @story ${STORY_PATH} */`;
    ```
  - Auto-fix for functions (uses `fix` field so ESLint --fix applies it):
    ```ts
    function reportMissing(context: Rule.RuleContext, sourceCode: any, node: any, passedTarget?: any): void {
      ...
      if (hasStoryAnnotation(sourceCode, node)) {
        return; // do not report/fix if annotation already present
      }
      const resolvedTarget = passedTarget ?? resolveTargetNode(sourceCode, node);
      const name = functionName;
      const nameNode = ...;

      context.report({
        node: nameNode,
        messageId: "missingStory",
        data: { name, functionName: name },
        fix: createAddStoryFix(resolvedTarget),
        suggest: [
          {
            desc: `Add JSDoc @story annotation for function '${name}', e.g., ${ANNOTATION}`,
            fix: createAddStoryFix(resolvedTarget),
          },
        ],
      });
    }
    ```
  - Auto-fix for methods (`reportMethod`) similarly uses `createMethodFix` and preserves surrounding structure.
- Low-level fix insertion logic in `src/rules/helpers/require-story-core.ts` shows conservative, minimal edits:
  ```ts
  export function createAddStoryFix(target: any) {
    function addStoryFixer(fixer: any) {
      const start = ... // safely derive start from parent or node range, default 0
      return fixer.insertTextBeforeRange([start, start], `${ANNOTATION}\n`);
    }
    return addStoryFixer;
  }

  export function createMethodFix(node: any) {
    function methodFixer(fixer: any) {
      const start = ...;
      return fixer.insertTextBeforeRange([start, start], `${ANNOTATION}\n  `);
    }
    return methodFixer;
  }
  ```
- These fixers:
  - Only insert text (no deletions/replacements of existing code), preserving runtime behavior.
  - Use comment constants and simple newlines, leaving existing indentation/structure intact.
  - Are only applied when `hasStoryAnnotation` is false, ensuring no duplicate or destructive edits.

3) Implementation of format auto-fix (REQ-AUTOFIX-FORMAT, SAFE, PRESERVE) for @story paths
- `src/rules/valid-annotation-format.ts` ties story 008.0 to format-fixing helpers:
  - `createStoryFix` calculates a minimal replacement range for the `@story` value:
    ```ts
    function createStoryFix(context: any, comment: any, fixed: string): null | (() => any) {
      const sourceCode = context.getSourceCode();
      const commentText = sourceCode.getText(comment);
      const search = "@story";
      const tagIndex = commentText.indexOf(search);
      // @req REQ-AUTOFIX-SAFE - Skip auto-fix when @story tag cannot be reliably located
      if (tagIndex === TAG_NOT_FOUND_INDEX) {
        return null;
      }
      ...
      const fixRange: [number, number] = [
        start + valueStartInComment,
        start + valueEndInComment,
      ];

      return () => (fixer: any) => fixer.replaceTextRange(fixRange, fixed);
    }
    ```
    - If `@story` tag cannot be found or the value range can’t be determined, it returns `null` (no fix), satisfying the “safe only” requirement.
  - `reportInvalidStoryFormatWithFix` decides whether to apply a fix or just report an error:
    ```ts
    function reportInvalidStoryFormatWithFix(context: any, comment: any, collapsed: string, fixed: string): void {
      const fixFactory = createStoryFix(context, comment, fixed);
      if (!fixFactory) {
        reportInvalidStoryFormat(context, comment, collapsed, getResolvedDefaults());
        return;
      }

      context.report({
        node: comment as any,
        messageId: "invalidStoryFormat",
        data: { details: buildStoryErrorMessage("invalid", collapsed, getResolvedDefaults()) },
        fix: fixFactory(),
      });
    }
    ```
  - `validateStoryAnnotation` uses `getFixedStoryPath` and only applies auto-fix when it yields a valid pattern match:
    ```ts
    const fixed = getFixedStoryPath(collapsed);
    if (fixed && pathPattern.test(fixed)) {
      reportInvalidStoryFormatWithFix(context, comment, collapsed, fixed);
      return;
    }
    reportInvalidStoryFormat(context, comment, collapsed, options);
    ```
- Rule meta explicitly limits fix scope:
  ```ts
  meta: {
    ...,
    /**
     * This rule's fixable support is limited to safe @story path suffix normalization per Story 008.0.
     * Fixes are limited strictly to adjusting the suffix portion of the @story path (e.g., adding
     * `.md` or `.story.md`), preserving all other comment text and whitespace exactly as written.
     *
     * @story docs/stories/008.0-DEV-AUTO-FIX.story.md
     * @req REQ-AUTOFIX-SAFE
     * @req REQ-AUTOFIX-PRESERVE
     */
    fixable: "code",
  },
  ```
- The passing tests in `tests/rules/valid-annotation-format.test.ts` and `tests/rules/auto-fix-behavior-008.test.ts` confirm behavior:
  - From Jest output:
    - "[REQ-AUTOFIX-FORMAT] adds .md extension for .story path"
    - "[REQ-AUTOFIX-FORMAT] adds .story.md extension when missing entirely"
  - Both verify that only the suffix is changed and the rest of the comment remains intact.

4) Error-handling behavior for unsafe/ambiguous cases (Error Handling acceptance criterion)
- For @story format fixes:
  - `createStoryFix` returns `null` when it cannot safely locate the `@story` value, and `reportInvalidStoryFormatWithFix` then falls back to a non-fix report.
  - Comments in code explicitly tie this to REQ-AUTOFIX-SAFE:
    ```ts
    // @req REQ-AUTOFIX-SAFE - Fall back to reporting without fix when safe fix cannot be created
    ```
- For missing @story annotations on functions/methods:
  - `reportMissing` / `reportMethod` in helpers are wrapped in `try { ... } catch { /* noop */ }` to avoid throwing on unusual AST/source conditions; worst case, no fix is applied and only existing validations remain.
  - `hasStoryAnnotation` uses multiple heuristics and is also guarded by try/catch, ensuring it never throws while trying to detect existing annotations; if anything is uncertain, the rule remains safely conservative.

5) Documentation of auto-fix behavior (Documentation acceptance criterion)
- In `user-docs/api-reference.md`, for `traceability/require-story-annotation`:
  ```md
  Description: Ensures every function declaration has a JSDoc comment with an `@story` annotation ...
  When run with `--fix`, the rule inserts a single-line placeholder JSDoc `@story` annotation above missing functions, methods, TypeScript declare functions, and interface method signatures using a built-in template aligned with Story 008.0. This template is currently fixed but structured for future configurability, and fixes are strictly limited to adding this placeholder annotation without altering the function body or changing any runtime behavior. Selective enabling of different auto-fix behaviors ... is planned for a future version.
  ```
- For `traceability/valid-annotation-format` in the same file:
  ```md
  When run with `--fix`, the rule limits changes to safe `@story` path suffix normalization only—for example, adding `.md` when the path ends with `.story`, or adding `.story.md` when the base path has no extension—using targeted replacements implemented in the `getFixedStoryPath` and `reportInvalidStoryFormatWithFix` helpers. It does not change directories, infer new story names, or modify any surrounding comment text or whitespace, in line with Story 008.0; more advanced path normalization strategies and selective toggles to enable or disable specific auto-fix behaviors are not yet implemented.
  ```
- These docs:
  - Explicitly describe what `--fix` does for each rule.
  - Call out the safety constraints and limited scope, matching REQ-AUTOFIX-SAFE/PRESERVE and the acceptance criterion about documentation.
  - Clearly indicate that template configurability and selective fix toggles (REQ-AUTOFIX-TEMPLATE, REQ-AUTOFIX-SELECTIVE) are not yet implemented, aligning with the story’s own notes.

6) Scope alignment with story requirements
- Implemented requirements per story text and code/tests:
  - REQ-AUTOFIX-MISSING: Implemented via `require-story-annotation` + helpers with placeholder template; covered by dedicated tests in `tests/rules/auto-fix-behavior-008.test.ts` (multiple node types).
  - REQ-AUTOFIX-FORMAT: Implemented via `valid-annotation-format` + `getFixedStoryPath` + `reportInvalidStoryFormatWithFix`; tested in `auto-fix-behavior-008.test.ts` and rule-specific tests.
  - REQ-AUTOFIX-SAFE / REQ-AUTOFIX-PRESERVE: Satisfied by conservative insertion-only fixes for missing annotations and narrow suffix-only replacements for format issues, with explicit guards that skip fixes when not safe.
- Explicitly *not* in scope for this iteration (and marked as such in the story):
  - REQ-AUTOFIX-TEMPLATE (configurable templates).
  - REQ-AUTOFIX-SELECTIVE (fine-grained toggles for different fix types).
  These are called out in both the story and documentation as future enhancements, and their absence does not contradict any checked acceptance criterion.

